@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@ from codemonkeys.prompts.shared_state_machine_prompts import format_files
 
 
 FULL_FILE_EXAMPLE = """
-Before editing, decompose the issue into atomic, externally observable requirements, including stated behavior, implied compatibility constraints, and any edge cases named or suggested by the report. For each requirement, trace the relevant call path from the public entry point through the implementation and record which code path must change and which existing behavior must remain unchanged. Do not implement until every requirement has a concrete verification plan. For every independently stated behavior, identify the implementation location, all relevant callers and consumers, and the compatibility contract they rely on. Run an ordinary-case test, a boundary or malformed-input test, and a regression test for the reported failure; also test at least one downstream consumer or serialization/API boundary when the changed value crosses a module boundary. Explain why the patch preserves unaffected callers before approval. Each test must assert the exact expected value, exception, state transition, or externally visible side effect—not merely that execution completes—and must exercise the public entry point used by the issue. For every failing test, classify the failure as an implementation defect, an incorrect expectation, an invalid test setup, or an environment problem; then make the smallest justified change and rerun the failed test plus the full affected requirement set. Never replace a failing assertion with a weaker existence or smoke check. Maintain a requirement-to-test matrix with one row per atomic requirement and columns for public entry point, input partition, expected result, observed result, and evidence. Mark each row PASS, FAIL, UNTESTED, or BLOCKED; never treat UNTESTED, BLOCKED, an infrastructure error, an empty test selection, or an ambiguous result as approval. Before finalizing, verify that every row has executed evidence and that boundary, malformed-input, regression, and unaffected-behavior partitions are represented where applicable. Do not approve a patch while any requirement lacks a directly relevant test or while a test result is ambiguous. When multiple candidate patches are available, compare them against the complete requirement matrix and consumer audit rather than selecting the most plausible explanation. Prefer a candidate only when it has direct passing evidence for every requirement, no unexplained regressions, and a clear causal explanation for the reported failure; reject candidates that pass only generic tests, rely on unverified assumptions, or leave any requirement unresolved. After each test run, map every failure, unexpected pass, skipped test, warning, and traceback back to a specific matrix requirement. For each result, record the observed behavior, expected behavior, likely root cause, and the next code or test change. Re-run the smallest reproducer after every revision, then re-run the full relevant suite. An unexplained failure, skipped requirement, or test whose assertion does not distinguish the buggy behavior from the fixed behavior blocks approval.
+Before editing, decompose the issue into atomic, externally observable requirements, including stated behavior, implied compatibility constraints, and any edge cases named or suggested by the report. For each requirement, trace the relevant call path from the public entry point through the implementation and record which code path must change and which existing behavior must remain unchanged. Do not implement until every requirement has a concrete verification plan. For every independently stated behavior, identify the implementation location, all relevant callers and consumers, and the compatibility contract they rely on. Run an ordinary-case test, a boundary or malformed-input test, and a regression test for the reported failure; also test at least one downstream consumer or serialization/API boundary when the changed value crosses a module boundary. Explain why the patch preserves unaffected callers before approval. Each test must assert the exact expected value, exception, state transition, or externally visible side effect—not merely that execution completes—and must exercise the public entry point used by the issue. For every failing test, classify the failure as an implementation defect, an incorrect expectation, an invalid test setup, or an environment problem; then make the smallest justified change and rerun the failed test plus the full affected requirement set. Never replace a failing assertion with a weaker existence or smoke check. Maintain a requirement-to-test matrix with one row per atomic requirement and columns for public entry point, input partition, expected result, observed result, and evidence. Mark each row PASS, FAIL, UNTESTED, or BLOCKED; never treat UNTESTED, BLOCKED, an infrastructure error, an empty test selection, or an ambiguous result as approval. Before finalizing, verify that every row has executed evidence and that boundary, malformed-input, regression, and unaffected-behavior partitions are represented where applicable. Do not approve a patch while any requirement lacks a directly relevant test or while a test result is ambiguous. When multiple candidate patches are available, score each candidate independently against every requirement and consumer audit using only observed evidence: required behavior passes, regression behavior passes, boundary and malformed-input behavior passes, unaffected behavior remains intact, and the diff is minimal and localized. Reject any candidate with a failing or untested required row; do not select a candidate because it is more eloquent, more complex, or preferred by a majority when its evidence is weaker. Prefer a candidate only when it has direct passing evidence for every requirement, no unexplained regressions, and a clear causal explanation for the reported failure; reject candidates that pass only generic tests, rely on unverified assumptions, or leave any requirement unresolved. After each test run, map every failure, unexpected pass, skipped test, warning, and traceback back to a specific matrix requirement. For each result, record the observed behavior, expected behavior, likely root cause, and the next code or test change. Re-run the smallest reproducer after every revision, then re-run the full relevant suite. An unexplained failure, skipped requirement, or test whose assertion does not distinguish the buggy behavior from the fixed behavior blocks approval.
 
 Before final approval, enumerate every caller, subclass, override, serializer, parser, and public consumer of each modified symbol using repository search, including indirect callers discovered through imports and dispatch tables. For each consumer, trace one representative input through the modified behavior and verify that the patch preserves its expected return type, exceptions, ordering, mutation, defaults, and backward compatibility. If any consumer has not been checked, do not approve the patch. For each consumer, record its expected inputs, outputs, exceptions, ordering, mutation, and compatibility invariant, then verify the patch against that record. Actively construct adversarial cases for empty values, null-like values, duplicate values, boundary sizes, invalid types, repeated calls, inheritance or override behavior, and serialization or deserialization. Explain why the implementation handles each applicable case and add a focused regression test for every case that could distinguish the patch from the pre-patch behavior. If any consumer cannot be located or its behavior is unknown, do not approve the patch: search broader call paths, add a focused regression test, or revise the implementation. Treat changed error types, defaults, ordering, mutation, and serialization as behavior changes requiring explicit justification.
 
